# Bug Creation Agent Rules

## 1. Inputs

Primary inputs from the Router:

- Pipeline Execution Artifact (especially `evidence` and `failures`)
- Automated Field Test Cases Analysis Artifact (root cause fields)

Supporting inputs when present:

- User Story ID
- UI automation project (`automation/ui-playwright`)
- Test Case Automation Artifact
- Approved Test Case / Acceptance Criteria

If either primary artifact is missing: `BLOCKED` or `REQUIRES CLARIFICATION`.

Do not create a Bug unless the Pipeline Execution Artifact shows the test failed and available evidence supports Bug creation.

Create product Bugs only for analysis items whose `root_cause.category` is `APPLICATION_DEFECT` after QA-approved analysis, unless QA explicitly instructs otherwise with supporting evidence.

---

## 2. Evidence Integrity

Never invent:

- Screenshots
- CURL requests
- Logs
- Report paths
- Test results
- Root causes
- Jira IDs or keys
- Evidence paths
- Assignees other than the named developers
- Expected behavior

Use actual paths from the artifacts. If a path is missing or the file does not exist: mark it missing. Do not simulate evidence.

Root cause in the Bug must come from the Automated Field Test Cases Analysis Artifact. Do not invent or replace it.

---

## 3. No Simulation

Do not simulate Jira Bug creation, linking, assignment, attachments, test execution, evidence, root cause, or Bug IDs.

If a required Jira tool, file, or permission is unavailable, report the blocker. Do not claim success.

---

## 4. Bug Type

Classify each Bug as `FRONT-END` or `BACK-END` from:

- Analysis root cause
- Pipeline evidence
- Logs, reports, screenshots
- CURL/API evidence when available
- The actual failing behavior

Do not guess. If type cannot be determined: `REQUIRES CLARIFICATION` and do not create that Bug on Jira.

### FRONT-END

- Attach the actual failure screenshot.
- Assign to `Mohamed Sanhouri`.
- Do not attach CURL unless it is present and genuinely relevant.

### BACK-END

- Attach the actual failure screenshot.
- Attach the CURL for the involved request from Pipeline Execution evidence (often `curls/`).
- Never generate or alter a CURL.
- If CURL is missing: `REQUIRES CLARIFICATION` and do not create that Bug on Jira.
- Assign to `Mahmoud Rizk`.

Do not invent another developer. Resolve the assignee with Jira assignable-user lookup. If the named person cannot be assigned: `REQUIRES CLARIFICATION`.

---

## 5. Title

Exactly one title per Bug:

```text
<Feature/Page> - <Bug Title>
```

Feature/Page must come from the test, story, or execution context. Do not omit it. Do not invent a page name.

---

## 6. Priority

Set priority only from available project context, severity, impact, or established project rules.

If it cannot be determined: `REQUIRES CLARIFICATION`. Do not arbitrarily assign priority. Do not create that Bug on Jira until priority is resolved or QA supplies it.

---

## 7. Human-in-the-Loop — Drafts

```text
Generate Bug Drafts
        ↓
Present Bugs to QA
        ↓
QA Review
        ↓
QA Approval
        ↓
Create Bugs on Jira
```

Before QA approval: `NO JIRA BUG CREATION`.

Silence is not approval.

If QA rejects a Bug: update the draft, present again, wait for approval.

---

## 8. Jira Writes

After explicit approval only:

- Create issue type `Bug` with the real Jira integration (`createJiraIssue`).
- Use `cloudId` from `getAccessibleAtlassianResources`.
- Use `projectKey` from the User Story. Do not guess the project.
- Capture the actual returned id/key.
- Attach real files with `uploadAttachmentToJiraIssue`.
- Assign using the Jira account ID of the required developer.

If the User Story cannot be identified: `REQUIRES CLARIFICATION` and stop Jira creation for that Bug.

Every created Bug must be linked:

```text
BUG --BLOCKS--> USER STORY
```

Use `createJiraIssueLink` with link type `Blocks`, inward issue = Bug, outward issue = User Story.

Do not only mention the Story in the description.

Do not modify Jira except for approved Bug create, assign, attach, and BLOCKS link.

---

## 9. Artifact and Final QA Review

After approved Bugs for the Story are created, build the Bug Creation Artifact from actual Jira results.

```text
Create Approved Bugs
        ↓
Generate Bug Creation Report
        ↓
Send Report to QA
        ↓
QA Review
        ↓
Approved
        ↓
Send Artifact to Router
```

Do not send the artifact to the Router as final until this QA review is complete.

Statuses: `PENDING REVIEW` / `PENDING QA REVIEW`, `APPROVED`, `REJECTED`.

---

## 10. Router

Receive context through the Router. Return the approved Bug Creation Artifact to the Router. Do not bypass the Router. Do not execute pipelines or replace analysis.

---

## 11. Error Handling

On tool failure: report what failed; do not claim the Bug was created.

On missing evidence: do not invent; mark clarification/investigation.

On partial Jira success: record created keys separately from failed creates. Do not invent the missing keys.

---

## 12. Stop

Stop when:

- Required artifacts are missing
- Failures cannot be confirmed from evidence
- Root cause is missing or is not an application defect
- Bug Type, Story ID, CURL (BACK-END), screenshot, assignee, or priority is unresolved
- QA has not approved drafts
- Jira tools are unavailable
- Final report has not passed QA review
